{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3370","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3370","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3370\",\"parent_revision\":\"5f90f3bd117e74c440c6f505c1337c69b9eecab03d0a54ec33bcd0f4fe9dd88f\",\"root_revision\":\"5f90f3bd117e74c440c6f505c1337c69b9eecab03d0a54ec33bcd0f4fe9dd88f\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-01\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nGoal: `write \u2192 render \u2192 parse` must be lossless for any section name, including one that narrowly misses an exact match against a registered canonical heading.\\n\\nReproduction: `tests/test_github_sync.py::TestSectionRoundTripProperty::test_write_render_parse_round_trip_is_lossless` (a hypothesis property test, `max_examples=200`) fails deterministically with the minimal case `section_name=':EFFORT'`, `content='## Fake Heading Inside Content'`:\\n\\n```\\nAssertionError: Key did not round-trip for section_name=':EFFORT': expected ['unknown__effort'], got ['effort', 'unknown__effort']\\n```\\n\\nReproduce directly:\\n```\\nuv run pytest \\\"plugins/development-harness/tests/test_github_sync.py::TestSectionRoundTripProperty::test_write_render_parse_round_trip_is_lossless\\\" -q --no-cov\\n```\\nConfirmed reproducible on 2 consecutive runs (not a one-off hypothesis flake \u2014 same failing example both times).\\n\\nWhat's happening (from reading the code, not yet root-caused with a debugger): `SectionKey.EFFORT = \\\"effort\\\"` is a registered canonical key with heading text \\\"Effort\\\" (`section_registry.py:86,119`). The caller-supplied name `\\\":EFFORT\\\"` doesn't exactly match the registered heading, so the write boundary stores it under the `unknown__` fallback convention (`rendering.py`'s `unknown__{normalised}` prefixing, confirmed by the stderr warning: \\\"Unregistered section name ':EFFORT' stored under fallback key 'unknown__effort'\\\"). But somewhere in the render\u2192parse leg, the *rendered* heading text for that fallback key evidently comes back out looking enough like the registered \\\"Effort\\\" heading that re-parsing resolves it to the plain canonical key `effort` \u2014 producing two keys for one logical section instead of one.\\n\\nThis is exactly the bug class `normalize_unknown_sections()` (`rendering.py:165`) was built to fold away, per its own docstring referencing #2956 (live data-loss defect: two keys for one heading, newer key's entries silently lost on next reparse) and #3015. That machinery evidently has a gap for this specific input shape \u2014 worth checking whether `normalize_unknown_sections` is even invoked on this code path, or whether the fallback-key-to-heading-text rendering for `unknown__effort` produces a heading string that re-resolves to `effort` on the next parse in a way the fold doesn't anticipate.\\n\\nImpact: per the existing #2956 docstring, this key-duplication pattern is a live data-loss risk (last-heading-wins on reparse can silently drop entries), not just a cosmetic duplicate heading \u2014 so this warrants investigation before being deprioritized as cosmetic.\\n\\nUser-provided context: not yet investigated with a debugger/print-tracing through the actual write\u2192render\u2192parse call chain for this specific input; the above is read-only code-reading, not a verified root cause. Whoever picks this up should trace `operations._normalize_section_key` (write boundary) and the GitHub-parse boundary's heading resolver against this exact input before proposing a fix.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Discovered incidentally by a hypothesis property test failure while working PR #3367 (unrelated typed-MCP-output work); not caused by that PR \u2014 confirmed none of its commits touch section_registry.py, rendering.py, or github_sync.py.\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-01\\n- **Research questions**: None\